# 1. 🌐 WHAT IS WEB DEVELOPMENT?

Web development means creating websites and web applications.

The three main technologies are:

HTML       → Structure 🧱
CSS        → Design 🎨
JavaScript → Behaviour / Interactivity ⚙️

### Real-Life Example

Think about a house:

HTML       → Walls, rooms, doors
CSS        → Paint, furniture, decoration
JavaScript → Lights, switches, actions

So remember:

HTML       → What is on the webpage?
CSS        → How does it look?
JavaScript → How does it work?

---

# 2. 🧱 WHAT IS HTML?

HTML stands for:

HyperText Markup Language

HTML is a markup language, not a programming language.

HTML is used to create the structure and content of webpages.

Example:

<h1>Hello World</h1>

The browser reads this and displays:

Hello World

---

# 3. 🏗️ BASIC HTML STRUCTURE

A basic HTML document looks like this:

<!DOCTYPE html>

<html>

<head>
    <title>My Website</title>
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Hello World</h1>

</body>

</html>

### Structure:

HTML
│
├── HEAD
│   └── TITLE
│
└── BODY
    └── H1

---

# 4. <!DOCTYPE html>

Syntax:

<!DOCTYPE html>

Purpose:

It tells the browser that the document uses HTML5.

Important:

It is a declaration, not a normal HTML element.

### Tip:

Always start a normal HTML5 page with:

<!DOCTYPE html>

---

# 5. <html>

Syntax:

<html>

</html>

Purpose:

<html> is the root element of the webpage.

Almost all HTML content is written inside <html>.

---

# 6. <head>

Syntax:

<head>

</head>

Purpose:

<head> contains information about the webpage.

Examples:

- Page title
- CSS
- Metadata
- External files

Most content inside <head> is not directly visible on the webpage.

---

# 7. <title>

Syntax:

<title>My First Website</title>

Purpose:

<title> controls the text shown in the browser tab.

Example:

<title>My Portfolio</title>

Browser tab:

My Portfolio

### Important Difference

<title>My Portfolio</title>

→ Browser tab

<h1>My Portfolio</h1>

→ Visible webpage

---

# 8. <body>

Syntax:

<body>

    Website content

</body>

Purpose:

<body> contains the content visible to the user.

Examples:

- Headings
- Paragraphs
- Images
- Links
- Lists
- Forms
- Buttons

---

# 9. 📝 HEADINGS

HTML provides six heading levels:

<h1>Heading 1</h1>
<h2>Heading 2</h2>
<h3>Heading 3</h3>
<h4>Heading 4</h4>
<h5>Heading 5</h5>
<h6>Heading 6</h6>

Common structure:

<h1> → Main topic
<h2> → Section
<h3> → Subsection
<h4> → Smaller subsection
<h5>
<h6>

Example:

<h1>My Portfolio</h1>

<h2>About Me</h2>

<h2>My Skills</h2>

<h3>Programming Skills</h3>

### Important Tip:

Do not use <h1>, <h2>, etc. only because you want bigger text.

Headings should represent the structure of your content.

CSS will later control the size and appearance.

---

# 10. 📝 PARAGRAPH <p>

Syntax:

<p>This is my first website.</p>

Purpose:

<p> is used to create a paragraph.

Example:

<h2>About Me</h2>

<p>
    I am a CSE student and I am learning Web Development.
</p>

---

# 11. 📋 UNORDERED LIST <ul>

An unordered list creates bullet points.

Example:

<ul>
    <li>Java</li>
    <li>SQL</li>
    <li>DSA</li>
    <li>HTML</li>
</ul>

Output:

• Java
• SQL
• DSA
• HTML

### Remember:

<ul> → Unordered List
<li> → List Item

### Memory Trick:

UL = Unordered List = Bullets

---

# 12. 🔢 ORDERED LIST <ol>

An ordered list creates numbered items.

Example:

<ol>
    <li>Learn HTML</li>
    <li>Learn CSS</li>
    <li>Learn JavaScript</li>
</ol>

Output:

1. Learn HTML
2. Learn CSS
3. Learn JavaScript

### Memory Trick:

OL = Ordered List = Numbers

---

# 13. 🔗 LINKS <a>

HTML uses <a> to create links.

Basic syntax:

<a href="https://github.com/">My GitHub</a>

### Parts:

<a>       → Creates a link
href      → Destination
Text      → Clickable text

General syntax:

<a href="URL">Clickable Text</a>

Example:

<a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>

---

# 14. 🔗 target="_blank"

To open a link in a new browser tab:

<a href="https://github.com/" target="_blank">
    My GitHub
</a>

Meaning:

target="_blank"

→ Open the link in a new tab.

### Best Practice:

For external links:

<a href="https://github.com/"
   target="_blank"
   rel="noopener noreferrer">
    My GitHub
</a>

rel="noopener noreferrer" is a useful security best practice when opening external links in a new tab.

---

# 15. 🖼️ IMAGES <img>

Images are added using:

<img src="myphoto.jpg" alt="My Profile Photo">

### Important Attributes:

src → Image location
alt → Alternative text

Example:

<img src="myphoto.jpg" alt="My Profile Photo">

### Important:

<img> does not need a closing </img> tag.

---

# 16. 📁 FILE PATHS

File paths are very important in web development.

Suppose the folder is:

Day1
│
├── index.html
└── myphoto.jpg

Then:

<img src="myphoto.jpg">

means:

"Look for myphoto.jpg in the same folder as index.html."

---

## Image inside another folder

Folder:

Day1
│
├── index.html
└── images
    └── myphoto.jpg

Then:

<img src="images/myphoto.jpg">

### Common mistakes:

myphoto.jpg
MyPhoto.jpg
myphoto.jpeg
myphoto.jpg.jpg

These are different filenames.

Always check the exact filename and extension.

---

# 17. ↩️ <br> — LINE BREAK

<br> moves content to the next line.

Example:

<p>
    I am a CSE student.<br>
    I am learning Web Development.<br>
    I am building projects.
</p>

Output:

I am a CSE student.
I am learning Web Development.
I am building projects.

### Memory Trick:

br = Break the line

---

# 18. ➖ <hr> — HORIZONTAL LINE

<hr> creates a horizontal line.

Example:

<h2>About Me</h2>

<p>I am learning Web Development.</p>

<hr>

<h2>My Skills</h2>

Output:

About Me

I am learning Web Development.

------------------------------

My Skills

### Memory Trick:

hr = Horizontal Rule

---

# 19. ⚠️ WHY DID WE USE <br><br>?

Example:

<br><br>

One <br>:

Name: [________]
Email: [________]

Two <br>:

Name: [________]


Email: [________]

So:

<br><br>

creates an extra blank line.

### Important:

In professional websites, CSS should normally control spacing.

We should not use many <br> tags just to create spacing.

We will learn proper spacing using CSS.

---

# 20. 🏷️ <label>

<label> tells the user what an input field is for.

Example:

<label>Name:</label>
<input type="text">

Output:

Name: [____________]

---

# 21. ⌨️ <input>

<input> creates a field where the user can enter information.

### Text Input:

<input type="text">

### Email Input:

<input type="email">

The email input tells the browser that the field is intended for an email address.

Other input types will be learned later.

---

# 22. 🔘 <button>

Creates a button.

Example:

<button>Submit</button>

Output:

[Submit]

### Important:

Our button does not actually submit data yet.

To make a real working form, we will later learn:

HTML Forms
JavaScript
Backend
Database

